It is reported that this is the second time that the research center has made a similar statement. In November last year, they tested 959 blood samples of lung cancer collected before the outbreak, and the results showed that 111 people were positive for COVID-19 antibody.
After learning this news, the World Health Organization asked Milan National Cancer Center to test samples again to verify the results. Subsequently, VisMederi Laboratory in Siena, Italy, and WHO affiliated institutions of Erasmus University in the Netherlands re-examined 29 original samples and control cases in 2018, and found that the coronavirus-related antibody IgM in 3 blood samples was positive, which was a sign of recent infection.
Although virus antibody was found, the antibody content of three samples did not meet the standard for confirming COVID-19 infection. Marion Copmans, a professor of virology at Erasmus University, thinks that the results are”interesting”and that it is”not impossible”for patients to be infected with COVID-19, but some evidence is needed.
