Although I love being Brazilian, I was really tempted to become an Estonian citizen. And that is only through the airport reception.
As we left the airport and entered the tram (a kind of train powered by electricity) we were looking for a place to buy tickets. Unfortunately, the information sign was only in Estonian, and the cell phone has no signal.
And he was determined to find out how he paid for the ticket. So I went to the driver and asked how much it was.
He asked me how many tickets and I signaled 2. He gave me 2 tickets and I went back to our place a little surprised at how the driver could not have known the value and what the money was about?
How can free public transport earn revenue for the city?
In a measure of social inclusion, to allow any citizen to use public transport free of charge, especially those who were looking for a job and who were in a more vulnerable financial situation, this free transport measure was adopted in the Estonian capital. The city served as a public policy laboratory.
I confess that I was happy to discover this. But then how does this public transport sustain itself?
The State Government of Estonia transfers to the City Hall (local government) a budget for public policies. This transfer is made by counting the inhabitants by municipality.
And why was this project created?
8% reduction in the use of private cars.
And not because of the tourists who pay for the tickets. And this is an experience that few people have experienced to see how citizens behave in a place where public transport is free for them.
I guarantee there is much more than good ice cream there.
So we keep bringing you this free content too.
