Why do the French twist their noses when you speak English and what has this to do with the Brazilians?
Well, almost all the people I've talked to about France, and who have been there, have always said two things:
And that the French twist their noses if you get English speaking.
The first statement is entirely true. In fact, France is a wonderful place and Paris represents it well.
Now let's go to the second statement, about the French twisting their nose if you get English speaking. On this I can say that it is partially true.
Based on my first trip to France I honestly can only say that I found the French very friendly, hardworking and hospitable. And actually twisting your nose if you get English speaking is actually a language barrier.
The truth is that English is a very different language from French. And in France they speak French.
Most Brazilians would not understand what he said and would "twist their nose" and look like: Huh ?
This is what happened during the 2014 world cup and the 2016 Olympics. what was seen was a flood of tourists surprised because they could not communicate in English and sometimes in Spanish (or Castilian).
And we arrived a little after lunch. That is, we were hungry and still with the suitcases looking for the apartment.
He called the other one who understood English. They then explained to me how it worked.
And we went through a lot of places and we were always treated with a "Bonjour" (good morning), "Bonsoir" (good evening) "Bon appétit" (good appetite). Sometimes with gestures and pointing what we wanted we managed to ask for things.
A hug and next time I'll talk about Paris with tips on places.
