What should we expect from Berlin, the capital of Germany?
In short, the city breathes history, since it was the epicenter where the events of the first and second world wars occurred. Truth be told, Berlin has come to change the history of humanity across the planet and is responsible for social, cultural and economic transformations, until we reach civilization as we know it today.
The fall of the Berlin wall in 1989 shows a reconnection of the city, of Germany, the reunion of relatives and the overcoming of a dark period, lived all over the world. I dare say it would be equivalent to the end of Apartheid in South Africa.
Nowadays, it is one of the places with the greatest crowds of tourists. Everyone wants to take a picture of the house and the famous sign saying "You are leaving the American sector"!
A TV tower (Fernsehturm) which is in this square is giant, has 368 meters and is the largest structure in Berlin. It's a broadcasting tower.
The monumental Brandenburg Gate was one of the gates that gave access to Berlin when the city was still small and surrounded by a wall, a kind of fortress. Today is probably the most famous postcard in Berlin.
This would be the equivalent of "Central Park" in New York or the "City Park in Brasilia". It is worth visiting for those who want to take a break and breathe fresh air amidst so many historical buildings in the city of Berlin.
Few people know but there is a phantom subway station in Berlin. There are no subway cars, no boarding or disembarkation.
And what happened?
It was the place where Hitler assembled the summit of his government and planned his strategies of war.
Given the historical importance of this place and the burden it brings, the new Germany decided that it would be better to demolish the bunker and build a square on it. This was a way of bringing a new meaning and giving space back to society.
Even the light carries a little of it.
It marks the East Berlin, this symbol can give you an idea of where you are in the city. In the western part the traffic lights were changed to the most common standard.
Karl Peglau created the Ampelmann to be "fluffy and functional". The two dolls (from the stop and pedestrian walk) were designed to improve the visual field of people with low vision.
No other traffic light symbol in the world was as carefully developed under technical and psychological aspects as the East Ampelmännchen. The life of its creator is linked to that of Berlin.
Any other place that should be on this list?
