What if I tell you that on our own (humans) the earth has gained a seventh continent?
And this material floats in the ocean and comes together until it forms what some researchers have called a "new continent." That's right, a plastic continent.
The main materials found would be linked by nylon threads, coming from fishing gear and clothing, which eventually join many other materials with them. Materials include for example bottles of all types, from pet to glass bottles, glasses, various plastic pots and their lids as well.
Well, call it plastic island or seventh continent the fact is that the problem exists and needs to be tackled. After all, if animals are feeding on plastic, and it does not degrade along the food chain, guess where it will focus?
The official name for this plastic putty is: Large Portion of Pacific Garbage.
So if you recycle, throw your bottle of water and other plastic objects in a proper place and not on the beach, or at least give it a proper destination. Doing this will help to save marine life.
“You never play outside, you only play inside!”
As gratitude, I leave a turtle for you to become aware of. She thanks you for your gesture not to throw away (or in any way end up at sea) the plastic, but to reuse, recycle or ship to a correct destination.
Well, in the next post I'll talk about the eighth continent, and it's closer to you than you think!
