Barcelona is great for those who like unusual architecture.
If this is your first trip to Barcelona follow these tips:
The first thing you will notice is that in Barcelona they do not speak Spanish, Catalan is their official language.
Similarly if you know Spanish you will also have some ease with Catalan. After all the language seems to be a kind of variant of Castilian.
Considering that you are wanting to see the dancing water show and already in Plaza de Catalunya has a lookout with the most breathtaking view of the city worth checking out, even you can have a panoramic view of various monuments, park, places, and castles there. It has a lift that is charged uphill, this helps reduce the amount of people up there.
5- Expiatory Temple of the Holy Family
Joining the Gothic style with the modernist, with his own personal touch ie three sets applied in the creation of the same place, considering that Gaudí had his own style and sought to work the materials of architectural creation. For this reason the Sagrada Familia is a church that deserves to be appreciated, starting from the outside.
For financial reasons Gaudí could not complete it, but used it as an experiment for the composition of his materials in other works. Worth the visit.
Still talking about the churches, there is the Gothic cathedral of Barcelona where you can see various graves including the tomb of Santa Eulalia. It's funny how in the old days people were literally buried inside the cathedrals.
From the ferryboat from Puerto.
Casa Batló is very different from anything you have ever seen, and its design also has the influence of Gaudí. When we went it was closed for renovation so we could only see the facade of the building.
If you are a football fan and like today's top players then you have to go to Camp Nou, better known as Barcelona stadium.
Worth the visit, the panoramic photo, but the store prices are a bit salty, the suggestion is to go to know, not to buy.
Now you can make a script to visit: “La ciudad Condal”, (City of the counts) Due to the participation of the Counts in their independence in the XNUMXth and XNUMXth centuries. Barcelona was the capital of the Kingdom of Catalonia and Aragon (Renato Aragão has a coat of arms, you see ?
Depending on where you arrive and what your next destination is I found this site that is well organized and has several transportation options including ships leaving from Barcelona.
