We interviewed a Mexican woman and brought Coronavirus Recommendations and Tips on Mexico Post navigation
And she joined our tour group and we shared the same table. Érika was a little curious about how international our table was.
And that's how I met Erika D. Barron that I'm going to interview today.
I was born in a small town in northern Mexico called Tampico in the State of Tamaulipas. But since 7 years ago I moved to the capital, Mexico City.
I work at a pharmaceutical company as a medical manager.
The majority of reported coronavirus infection cases are mild illnesses. And the serious ones occurring in 16% of the cases.
This includes basic care such as: washing your hands, not coughing on top of others, or wearing a mask if you are infected?
(After all this is a travel blog). We thank you for bringing all this clarification about the coronavirus.
There are many things that I like in Mexico, I would be between culture and food.
If I could, I would change the allocation of government resources and channel them into education and health.
(Particularly on my first trip to Mexico I was fascinated by the Mayan pyramids, and the beaches in Cancún, Tulum ... I couldn't say which is the best).
10- And I know you lived in Brazil for a while!
And did the Brazilians treat you well?
She even helped to translate things for the waiter. She is amazing!
The Brazilians treated me very well, they even tried to speak “portunhol” (Portuguese + Spanish) which helped a little for those who could not speak Portuguese. hehe.
Not really, you should be proud of your culture.
Anything else you want to share, to be recorded on this blog?
28-February international day of rare diseases. (it's the symbol that is drawn on Erika's hand)
