Interview with a Brazilian, how is the experience of an exchange?
To do this I will count on Thereza's help.
She participated in the “Science without Borders” program and had a great experience studying and living in Italy.
Here we go? We begin with a brief presentation:
- Hello, first I would like to say that it is an immense pleasure to be sharing a little bit of my experience with you.
- Why did you choose to spend this time studying?
When I was in graduate school, the Science Without Borders program emerged, and then it was a matter of opportunity, opportunity arose and I took it. I always thought about having an exchange experience, but I would definitely look for a program to have a scholarship and make this dream more financially viable.
- What about language, did you have any barriers with language?
And Portugal had great institutions in my area. I signed up and passed all the stages, however, I still don't know why these scholarships for Portugal were canceled, I don't know if I had a lot of students, or for what reason.
- What did you like most about Italy?
Look, I even wanted to visit Florence, Thereza speaks with such passion that I will definitely put it in the itinerary when it is time to visit Italy. One city I met and also fell in love with for the same reasons was Riga!
- What would you change there if you could? - The bureaucracy.
- How was the acceptance of the university that received you?
- What would you recommend to a tourist visiting Italy for the first time?
- How did you get back to Brazil?
- What about the job market?
- Yes, I am not working in my training area, but having an exchange in the curriculum does, whether because of contacts, training, experience, language.
- In my case, the exchange made me a more informal person than I already was, I identify with small companies, where the excellence of the work is more important than the profit. I believe in participatory management, employee autonomy and artisanal work.
We are at the very end of the interview and here is the space I usually open for anyone who wants to leave a message for the world or any thoughts. Any more Thereza messages?
Above all, 1 year away from your usual life is to be sure, convinced, that not everything can be the same when you return, perhaps unpleasant surprises will happen on your return, you never know what might happen. A year away is giving up the people around you hoping that they will understand your absence, and hoping even more that they are waiting for you in return.
To spend a year away and enjoy this time you need, above all, courage, will, a bit of a wooden face, a lack of shame and the will to live learning, discovering, breaking your face, making mistakes and getting it right. Of course, the adventurous spirit and the desire to know the world must be inherent in every exchange student.
There are those who manage to kill homesickness with memories ... learn to live for yourself, learn to walk in the rhythm of your context. Finally, be aware of what awaits you!
And here we end our interview.
