The Mayans and the forecast of the end of the world - Mexico Post navigation
They had their own number system
They were true lords of time.
And what's the fun in that?
What makes this Mayan calendar about 8,6 months old. Others claim that 260 days is the time it takes for the sun to appear twice at the same fixed point in the sky.
This would be the closest to our annual calendar.
Multiplying 18 x 20 = 360 days. (And there you are bragging about receiving the thirteenth salary!
Hahaha). And where are the 5 missing days of the year?
Thus, the Maya time counting took place with the 260 x 365 day calendar and the combinations that would take about 52 years to be able to repeat themselves again. It is interesting to note that 52 years was more or less the life expectancy of an individual.
They are not called lords of time for nothing. In order to record events, harvests and to be able to record history, they created a larger wheel, the so-called Baktun (b'ak'tun) that recorded approximately 400 years (394,3 years more exactly)
Transcribing all that time and cycles to our Gregorian calendar, we reach the date of December 21, 2012. Which is nothing more than the alignment of the 3 calendars starting a new cycle of about 400 years.
