Many people have heard of Marie Curie, especially since she won not only 1, but 2 Nobel Prizes in different areas, in Physics (in 1903) and Chemistry (1911).
The beginning of the saga.
To enter the doctorate it was necessary to find an advisor and would have to be someone special, without these prejudices and social limitations of the time. It turns out that this someone existed and was already eyeing the prodigy woman.
Together the couple researched about radioactivity, a term she invented, based on the studies of another Frenchman, Henri Becquerel, about a strange form of uranium energy.
For the work developed by the trio: the couple Curie and Henri won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1903, and thus Marie Curie became the first married woman, mother of two daughters to win a Nobel Prize. The Second Nobel
The children, well, they alternated between being with their mother, with their father, Pierre, with an aunt ... But the research continued and with its nose for news, and if their calculations were correct, there was something that emitted radiation 400 times larger and another 800 times larger than uranium.
If on the one hand there was a tragedy, on the other hand there was a greater approximation between the mother and her daughters. And also the trust in other people who helped to take care of the little ones while she finished her research.
Her daughter Irene continued her studies in chemistry and…
Irene, just as the mother ended up falling in love with her lab assistant, they also got married and became known as the Joliot-Curie couple and together they discovered that from the work of Marie and Pierre, who isolated radioactive elements what happens naturally, they achieved the alchemical dream of transforming one chemical element into another. This fact won the third Nobel prize for the Curie Family, now in the form of Joliot-Curie
You still remember Eve, Marie Curie's second daughter, right?
Not suffering from the effects of radiation. Now, in Warsaw, Poland, we have the Marie Sklodowska-Curie immortalized near the university that she so wanted to study and teach…
As Marie Curie has always known, from her parents until the end of her life, family and friends are the most important support you can have in life.
Find out who to make alliances with, surround yourself with those who dream of you or support your dreams, with those who have no prejudices.
And as we have seen, the mother's past legacy to her daughters, encouraging them, providing education, not having a closed heart for the pains of the past, but focusing on the future of life yields two more. In the end, the Curie family was right, 4 nobles in different areas would be a good start.
and wish you happy Mother's Day by encouraging them not to give up on their dreams. So Marie Skłodowska Curie, thank you for the learning you left us.
