These factors are taken into consideration by potential creditors (lending institutions, banks, etc. ), and also by potential vendors and even clients before deciding to grant credit, extend a loan, or engage your company in a business venture.
Assets: This is the single most important measure. What is your company worth?
How healthy is its balance sheet?
This is probably the most significant and most often considered factor when deciding whether your company or corporation is credit-worthy.
How reliably has your company repaid its loans in the past?
How much credit has been granted to your company?
All of these questions play a significant role in a creditor deciding on your company’s ability to pay its loans.
How healthy is your business?
What type of economic environment is it operating in?
Are there a significant number of judgments or liens against it?
The ability of your business to stay in business is also a very important factor that is considered by others when you are trying to establish business credit.
