In Wyoming, you can have a “nominee Director” or “Nominee manager.” This is an optional service we provide so that one of our associates will show up in the public records as Wyoming LLC manager instead of you, should you so wish. Wyoming LLC law states that the names of the LLC managers must be publicly filed.
Owners of privately held Wyoming LLCs and corporations do not need to have their names disclosed in the public records. This helps protect members and shareholders from lawsuits as the public cannot necessarily tie them to the company.
Along with this, here are some other tax benefits Wyoming offers:
Wyoming does not share business or personal records with outside agencies. Since Wyoming does not have an information sharing agreement with the IRS, this keeps a conflict between a state and federal computer from creating a red flag.
Aside from being 18 and older, there are few regulations about who can form a Wyoming LLC. This makes forming an LLC in Wyoming an ideal choice for those seeking to take advantage of Wyoming’s asset protection benefits.
A one-person LLC offers charging order protection. So, when an owner is sued the law can protect the member from losing the company or the assets inside.
The business mindset of Wyoming makes it appealing to many looking to start a business. Offering low taxation, privacy, and protection, many agree that Wyoming is the most favorable state in which to form an LLC.
So, those are the two minor disadvantages or cons with Wyoming LLCs that come to mind. So by and large, with the protection and the low initial and annual costs, there are many more advantages to disadvantages with Wyoming LLCs.
