Fast broadband connections have opened up a whole new way of getting content, spawning a plethora of streaming services.
Not all streaming services are created equally.
Prices range from £6 to £9 based on how many screens you want to watch at the same time, as well as the resolution of the stream, which hits 4K at the top end as long as your internet connection and devices can handle it.
There’s an app or way to stream Netflix on pretty much any device available, although some older smart TVs are the exception.
The company’s content catalogue varies by country.
Netflix has a few big name new TV shows backed only by older or less popular shows.
Its library is different in the UK compared to the US, for instance, but it combines a subscription service with a movie and TV show rental or buying service.
The UK library of popular shows is different in content but comparable in size to Netflix. Highlights include Mr Robot, Alpha House and Transparent.
TV shows and movies can be stored for offline viewing on iOS devices.
Entertainment includes Sky’s traditional channels and some others that are not available on Freeview, as well as a fairly large collection of box set television shows, including current hit TV shows.
Sport provides live access to seven Sky Sports channels.
Now TV’s sports offering is almost unique in providing live streaming access to premium sport, with only BT Sport providing similar.
It is particularly apparent with sport, as the frame rate is noticeably poorer than traditional broadcast.
But its support isn’t quite as diverse as Netflix.
The days of requiring an aerial or satellite dish to sate your TV and movie-watching needs are long gone.
Verdict: best cross-platform support and streaming quality, library in the UK is relatively small
Amazon’s video streaming service also varies by country.
For those who subscribe to Amazon’s Prime service, users get access to a subset of movies and TV shows that often includes the first couple of seasons of a TV.
Its movie selection has several streaming exclusives, but as with Netflix they often take a while to appear.
Amazon supports streaming via several consoles, its own Fire TV and tablet devices, iOS devices and in-browser support.
Verdict: a decent streaming subscription, with additional pay-for options making it the most comprehensive service
Movies includes Sky movies and roughly 1,000 movies for on-demand viewing, including many that appear well before they do on other subscription services.
Sky’s support of devices is relatively good, with decent Android, iOS and console apps, support for Roku, some of LG’s smart TVs and computers using Microsoft’s Silverlight.
Verdict: expensive, but has content you either can’t get or gets way before other subscription services
As well as Now TV and Sky Go, which is only available as part of a satellite subscription, Sky also has a separate movie rental and purchase service called Sky Store.
Movies bought through Sky Store give the user access to an HD stream of the movie, plus a DVD sent to their home in the post.
Movies can be watched through a variety of tablets, computers and streaming boxes, but the quality varies.
Verdict: a solid library of new and popular ad-hoc rentals and purchases with good multi-platform support, but no 5.1 or 1080p
You can download content for offline viewing on tablets and smartphone, but HD content can’t be played via the website, smartphone or tablet apps.
Verdict: cheaper, with 5.1 sound and in better quality than some pay-per-view rivals but with support for fewer devices
TV shows and movies arrive at about the same time as Google Play and other pay-per-view services.
