Microsoft Surface Book 2 review: a powerful yet pricey laptop-tablet combo
The latest Surface Pro 4 is thinner and more capable, but pretty much the same: a Windows 10 tablet with a removable keyboard.
It sounds like an odd distinction, but it makes quite a difference if the keyboard is your primary driver of a computer.
But it can also have a discrete graphics card within it, depending on the model, giving the Surface Book much more grunt when required.
It looks weird, almost cool, and in practice works well.
The keyboard and screen are made from a grey magnesium alloy, which looks and feels nice, but marks quite easily.
When shut, the hinge does not fold flat, instead it has a gap about a finger’s width creating a wedge shape that tapers from 22.8mm to a point of 13mm thick.
The software doesn’t really have any particular tweaks or customisations for the Surface Book that aren’t available on any other Windows 10 convertible.
But when you need to change something serious, uninstall a desktop program for instance, you get booted back into the same Control Panel I remember from Windows 2000 – it has barely changed.
For the uninitiated I suspect it could be quite baffling, particularly if you’re using your finger at the time and suddenly have to tap on tiny boxes.
The first is Windows Hello, which recognises your face and logs you in without passwords, is fantastic and works as quickly as the computer can boot.
The Surface Book has a beautiful, high resolution and pixel-dense screen that has great viewing angles and looks brilliant, until you reach an app that can’t scale properly.
Apps from the Windows store don’t suffer from the same problem, but they’re often not as powerful.
Some Windows laptops are thinner, some are lighter, but none have quite as good a combination of screen, keyboard, power and industrial design.
But the Surface Book is a different animal altogether: a laptop first, tablet second.
First and foremost, the Surface Book is the best Windows laptop I have ever used.
The tablet is held onto the keyboard by a very fancy “memory wire” locking mechanism, which changes shape when a current is applied across it.
It has two front-facing speakers that are loud and clear for watching video, while the headphones port is in the tablet, not the keyboard.
Despite being a full-blooded PC it’s still only 7.7mm thick, which is really quite impressive.
