Perhaps you don’t like the name Alexa – or perhaps you have a family member of that name and don’t want to cause confusion.
To do so, open the Alexa app on your smartphone or tablet, go to settings, then click on your Echo and tap to change the “wake word”.
You may also wish to go into sounds & notifications and enable audio feedback – a gentle “boop” – when the Echo hears Alexa’s name, so you know when it’s listening.
To connect your Google calendar, simply go into settings in the Alexa app, then tap calendar>Google calendar>link Google calendar account.
Just be warned that Alexa doesn’t have any concept of privacy: if you have any secret rendezvous noted down in your Google calendar, don’t be surprised if she spills the beans to an inquisitive family member.
The Echo setup process prompts you to provide your home address, so Alexa can immediately offer local weather and news reports.
To do this, open the Alexa app, go to settings>traffic and tap to enter your work address, along with any relevant waypoints along the route.
In some cases, this may require you to equip Alexa with a new “skill”: to do so, go to skills>categories>smart home and search for your device.
You can then issue commands such as “Alexa, set the Nest temperature to 20 degrees” or “Alexa, brighten the bedroom light”.
If you have an Amazon Prime account that’s set up for one-click ordering, you can enable voice purchasing within the Alexa app.
You can also add items to your basket for later review.
The Echo can also play songs from your personal Amazon music library – you can upload up to 250 tracks free.
If you don’t want to specify the source every time, tap “Choose default music services” and choose Spotify from the menu that appears.
The Echo doesn’t have an FM receiver, but it integrates with TuneIn to provide access to a huge range of radio stations over the internet.
Since TuneIn is a global service, there’s a far wider range of stations to choose from than you’d get with a regular receiver – why not try WDDH in New York, or ABC Adelaide?
You can also ask Echo to play music from a particular genre.
If you’re a fan of the spoken word, you’ve probably built up a library of Audible audiobooks.
As with music, you can tell Alexa to pause and resume as needed: “go back” skips back 30 seconds, while “next chapter” and “previous chapter” work as you’d expect.
Her delivery won’t be as natural as a professional storyteller, though, and don’t expect her to do the voices.
Once you’ve installed the skill (and linked it to your Just Eat account), you can order dinner without having to lift a finger.
As with Amazon voice purchasing, there’s nothing to prevent the service from being abused by inquisitive children, but perhaps that won’t be a big problem for the single men it’s evidently aimed at.
After you’ve devoured a few takeaways, you may need a spot of exercise.
If you’re not sure how an exercise should be done, you can see a graphical tutorial within the Alexa app.
In future, the developers plan to extend the feature so you can track your performance and get access to a wider range of exercises.
Alexa can roll virtual dice for you too – with any number of sides – and she plays a mean game of rock, paper, scissors.
If you’re looking for more cerebral stimulation, try asking some philosophical questions, such as whether Santa Claus exists or who let the dogs out.
